    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c6eedb01db06a468f1261d47e1cd447c7983c6b35b7290a39edb45d30d06bba8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Logarithmic differentiation converts multiplicative product data into additive terms whose signs can control one-dimensional zero geometry. In the Eisenstein-integral argument, symmetry-block pairing and a coarse zero-height bound yield monotonicity; in the Speiser setting, a negative real logarithmic derivative rules out isolated critical-line derivative zeros and supplies vertical-boundary control for a matched contour count.",
